## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`options`): This is where the Contentstack SDK Stack is configured and initialized. The options object (line 71) and Stack creation (line 91) are the single point that controls which API (Delivery vs Preview) all content queries route through. Live Preview requires a live_preview config block in the Stack initialization — without it, every query (getEntry, getSingleEntry, findSingletonEntry, getSingletonEntry, and the ContentstackFactory queries) always hits the Delivery API and can never return draft/unpublished content for CMS preview. No downstream consumer (useContent, ContentstackFactory, components) can fix this — they all receive data from this Stack instance.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add a live_preview property to the options object (after line 75, before fetchOptions). Destructure two new env vars alongside the existing ones (around line 23-36): CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST and CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN. Then add to the options object: `live_preview: { preview_token: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN, host: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST }` — only when CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST is truthy, so non-preview deployments are unaffected. The Contentstack SDK (already imported as `contentstack` on line 2) natively handles Live Preview routing when this config is present: it switches from the Delivery API to the Preview API for any query made through the Stack instance, which is exactly what all existing query functions (getEntry, getSingleEntry, findSingletonEntry, getSingletonEntry, ContentstackFactory) already use. No new query logic is needed.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- With a piece of content open in the Contentstack CMS editor for the GO-branded site, a tester saves an unpublished change and observes the rendered GO page update to display the changed content without manually reloading the page.
- With a piece of content open in the Contentstack CMS editor for the UP-branded site, a tester saves an unpublished change and observes the rendered UP page update to display the changed content without manually reloading the page.
- With a piece of content open in the Contentstack CMS editor for the MX-branded site, a tester saves an unpublished change and observes the rendered MX page update to display the changed content without manually reloading the page.
- When the page is viewed outside a CMS preview session (live preview not connected), the page renders its content fully with no errors and no blank or missing sections.
- During a CMS preview session, a tester observes that content changes which have not been published are visible on the rendered page.
- A tester makes several rapid successive edits in the CMS editor and observes the rendered page reflect each change, ending with the most recent edit displayed and no stale, duplicated, or flickering content.

